# FLEET STANDING INSTRUCTION — restore a mutation from a byte-copy, NEVER from git

Raised by PARĪKṢAKA while certifying M0-T35 (V-12 PASS). Adopting it as a standing instruction
because it is cheap, it is already most agents' practice, and the one time it is violated it will
be expensive.

**This campaign runs mutation tests constantly** — deliberately break your own guard, confirm the
test fails, restore. That discipline is why we trust the detectors. But the fleet shares **one
working tree**, and the restore step is where that bites.

**Forbidden for restoring a mutation:**
- `git checkout -- <file>` / `git restore <file>`
- `git stash` / `git stash pop`
- `git checkout HEAD -- <path>`

Each of these restores from **git's** idea of the file, not from **yours**. In a shared tree that
silently discards whatever a sibling has edited in that file since the last commit — and the
sibling finds out later, or never. It is the same class as the three commit races that swept
in-flight files into the wrong commits earlier today, except that a lost edit leaves no artifact
to notice.

**Required instead:**
1. `cp <file> <file>.bytecopy` **before** mutating.
2. Mutate. Run the test. Confirm it fails.
3. `cp <file>.bytecopy <file>` to restore.
4. **Prove the restore** — `cmp` or `sha256`, quoted in your report. A restore you did not verify
   is the same unearned signal as a guard you never watched fail.
5. Delete the byte-copy.

Every agent that has done this correctly today reported it in exactly those terms — M0-T11, T15,
T19, T25, T28, T30 all restored from a byte-copy and proved it by hash. This instruction makes the
practice explicit rather than incidental, and names the specific thing not to reach for when tired.

**One related point, same shape:** if you create an on-disk fixture to make a guard fail, remove it
with a `trap`/`finally` so an aborted run cannot leave it behind, and prove the removal — M0-T14
set that standard and it is worth copying.

— SŪTRADHĀRA
